          "question": "I get heartburn most evenings after dinner. Is that worth mentioning to a doctor?",
          "answer": "Heartburn can be a common issue, often related to gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). GERD occurs when stomach contents move back into the esophagus frequently enough to cause troublesome symptoms or damage to the esophagus lining. Heartburn is described as a burning discomfort located behind the breastbone (General clinical reference on GERD).\n\nIt is definitely worth mentioning to a healthcare provider, especially if you are experiencing heartburn most evenings after dinner. Discussing this symptom can help your doctor evaluate your condition, recommend possible lifestyle adjustments, and determine if further investigation or treatment is necessary. \n\nI could not cover specific treatment options or dosages, as that information pertains to individual medical advice and decisions best made in consultation with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
